From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 08:38:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] msm8996-common: stop declaring the Bluetooth audio HIDL HAL

FCM 7 does not list it, so declaring it fails check_vintf:

  The following instances are in the device manifest but not specified in
  framework compatibility matrix:
    android.hardware.bluetooth.audio@2.1::IBluetoothAudioProvidersFactory/default

24.0's Bluetooth stack talks to the AIDL provider, so a HIDL 2.1 declaration
buys nothing even where the interface still builds: the audio service lists
2.2/2.1/2.0 as optional passthrough registrations and simply warns when they
fail. Removing the claim does not remove a capability the framework was
using.

Whether A2DP offload still works is a runtime question for the first boot,
not something this can settle. bonito's precedent, if it does not, is to move
the Bluetooth audio HAL to AIDL rather than restore this entry.

---
 manifest.xml | 9 ---------
 1 file changed, 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/manifest.xml b/manifest.xml
index daedfb9e..b1d91a84 100644
--- a/manifest.xml
+++ b/manifest.xml
@@ -26,15 +26,6 @@
             <instance>default</instance>
         </interface>
     </hal>
-    <hal format="hidl">
-        <name>android.hardware.bluetooth.audio</name>
-        <transport>hwbinder</transport>
-        <version>2.1</version>
-        <interface>
-            <name>IBluetoothAudioProvidersFactory</name>
-            <instance>default</instance>
-        </interface>
-    </hal>
     <hal format="hidl">
         <name>android.hardware.camera.provider</name>
         <transport>hwbinder</transport>
